One of the simplest and most useful density models is the Gaussian distribution, which in one dimension (1D) is: $$\begin{aligned} p_{\theta}(x) = \frac{1}{Z}e^{-(x-\theta_1)^2/(2\theta_2)}
\end{aligned}$$ This density has two parameters $\theta_1$
and $\theta_2$, which are the mean and variance of the distribution.

Assuming a pinhole camera and using the depth, $z \left[n,m \right]$, and the intrinsic camera parameters, $\mathbf{K}$, we obtain the 3D coordinates for a pixel with image coordinates $[n,m]$ in the camera reference frame as $$\begin{aligned}
\begin{bmatrix}
X \\
Y \\ Z \end{bmatrix}
= z\left[n,m \right] \times \mathbf{K}^{-1} \times
\begin{bmatrix}
n \\
m \\ 1 \end{bmatrix}
\end{aligned}$$

concepts.content:
A radiance field models a scene by assigning a color and volumetric density to each point in 3D space. It represents a portion of the plenoptic function based on the idea that the optical content of a scene can be modeled as a cloud of colorful particles with varying levels of transparency. This representation can be rendered into images using volume rendering.

A radiance field $L$ is defined as a mapping from scene coordinates to color and density (transparency) values. The coordinates can be 2D ($X,Y$) or 3D ($X,Y,Z$) and may include angular dimensions ($\psi$ and $\phi$) to represent the viewing angle. Representing color with $r,g,b$ and density with a scalar $\sigma$, the function is expressed as:

$$\begin{aligned}
    L: X,Y,Z,\psi,\phi \rightarrow r,g,b,\sigma \quad\quad\triangleleft \quad\text{radiance field}
\end{aligned}$$

The angular coordinates allow an object's color to vary depending on the viewing angle, which is necessary for modeling shiny surfaces. The subcomponents $L^c$ and $L^\sigma$ denote the outputs for color and density respectively:

$$\begin{aligned}
    L^c&: X,Y,Z,\psi,\phi \rightarrow r,g,b\\
    L^{\sigma}&: X,Y,Z,\psi,\phi \rightarrow\sigma
\end{aligned}$$

In a static scene where only the camera moves in a straight line with velocity $\dot{\mathbf{T}} = \mathbf{V} = (V_X,V_Y,V_Z)^\mathsf{T}$, the motion of a point in space $\mathbf{P} = (X,Y,Z)^\mathsf{T}$ relative to the camera is $\dot{\mathbf{P}} = -\mathbf{V}$. This is equivalent to all scene points moving with the same velocity relative to the camera. The resulting 2D motion field is given by:

$$\begin{aligned}
\begin{bmatrix}
\dot{x} \\
\dot{y}
\end{bmatrix}
= \frac{1}{Z}
\begin{bmatrix}
-f & 0 & x \\
0 & -f & y  
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
V_X\\
V_Y \\
V_Z 
\end{bmatrix}
\end{aligned}$$

This relationship can be rearranged to make the contribution of camera coordinates more explicit:

$$\begin{aligned}
\begin{bmatrix}
\dot{x} \\
\dot{y}
\end{bmatrix}
= -\frac{f}{Z}
\begin{bmatrix}
V_X\\
V_Y 
\end{bmatrix}
+
\frac{V_Z}{Z}
\begin{bmatrix}
x\\
y 
\end{bmatrix}
\end{aligned}$$

Lateral camera motion occurs when a camera translates parallel to the camera plane, such as when looking through a side window of a moving car. In this case, the forward velocity $V_Z=0$. (see figure: Lateral camera motion parallel to the camera plane.)

The relationship between the velocity of a 3D point and its apparent velocity in the image plane is:

$$\begin{aligned}
\begin{bmatrix}
\dot{x} \\
\dot{y}
\end{bmatrix}
= 
-\frac{f}{Z}
\begin{bmatrix}
 V_X \\
V_Y  
\end{bmatrix}
\end{aligned}$$

The motion field depends on the depth $Z$ at each location. Objects close to the camera appear to move faster than objects farther away, while very distant objects appear stationary. This parallax effect is used in stereo vision to recover depth. The 2D motion in the image plane is in the opposite direction to the camera motion.

(see figure: Sketch of the motion field under lateral camera motion. Objects close to the camera will appear to be moving faster than objects farther away. Objects that are very far (like the cloud) will appear...)

For a camera moving forward along the camera axis ($V_X = V_Y = 0, V_Z \neq 0$), the motion field is described by:

$$\begin{aligned}
\begin{bmatrix}
\dot{x} \\
\dot{y}
\end{bmatrix}
= 
\frac{V_Z}{Z}
\begin{bmatrix}
x \\
y  
\end{bmatrix}
\end{aligned}$$

(see figure: Camera moving forward, along the camera axis.)

The rate of expansion depends on the ratio $V_Z/Z$ (the inverse of the time to contact) and is independent of the focal length $f$. For a camera moving in an arbitrary direction ($V_X, V_Y, V_Z \neq 0$), the motion field is:

$$\begin{aligned}
\begin{bmatrix}
\dot{x} \\
\dot{y}
\end{bmatrix}
=
\frac{V_Z}{Z}
\begin{bmatrix}
x - x_{\infty}\\
y - y_{\infty} 
\end{bmatrix}
\end{aligned}$$

The observed motion is zero at the focus of expansion, $(x_{\infty}, y_{\infty})$. Points near this focus (the point of impact) appear stationary, while peripheral points move faster and away from the center, causing the image to expand over time.

(see figure: Sketch of the motion field when the camera approaches a planar surface. The motion field indicates the rate of expansion of the image, and it is a function of the time to contact. In this example...)

For a camera undergoing general motion with translation $\mathbf{V}$ and angular velocity $\mathbf{W} = (W_X, W_Y, W_Z)$, the velocity of a 3D point relative to the camera is $\dot{\mathbf{P}} = - \mathbf{V} - \mathbf{W} \times \mathbf{P}_t$. Using Euler angles (yaw, pitch, roll) and assuming small displacements over time $\Delta t$, the rotation matrix $\mathbf{R}_{\Delta t}$ can be approximated to derive the 2D motion field:

(see figure: Rotation expressed by Euler angles (yaw, pitch, roll).)

$$\begin{aligned}
\begin{bmatrix}
\dot{x} \\
\dot{y}
\end{bmatrix}
= \frac{1}{Z}
\begin{bmatrix}
-f & 0 & x \\
0 & -f & y  
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
V_X\\
V_Y \\
V_Z 
\end{bmatrix}
+
\frac{1}{f}
\begin{bmatrix}
xy & -f^2-x^2) & f y \\
f^2+y^2 & -xy & -f x  
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
W_X\\
W_Y \\
W_Z 
\end{bmatrix}
\end{aligned}$$

This expression relates the 2D motion field to camera velocity, rotation, intrinsic focal length $f$, and camera coordinates. It is valid for small displacements.

When a camera undergoes pure rotation ($V_X=V_Y=V_Z=0$), the motion field is:

$$\begin{aligned}
\begin{bmatrix}
\dot{x} \\
\dot{y}
\end{bmatrix}
= 
\frac{1}{f}
\begin{bmatrix}
xy & -f^2-x^2) & f y \\
f^2+y^2 & -xy & -f x  
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
W_X\\
W_Y \\
W_Z 
\end{bmatrix}
\end{aligned}$$

Because this field does not depend on depth $Z$, observing the motion field under pure rotation provides information about camera motion and parameters, but nothing about the 3D scene structure. For rotation around the optical axis ($W_X=W_Y=0$), the field points orthogonally to the vector from the origin and is independent of focal length $f$. (see figure: Motion field for a rotating camera around the optical axis, $W_X=W_Y=0$.)

For rotation around the $Y$-axis ($W_X=W_Z=0$), the focal length $f$ significantly affects the field's appearance. At large $f$, the motion appears constant and similar to lateral translation; at small $f$, it resembles a homography. (see figure: Motion flows corresponding to a rotation around the Y-axis. (left) f = 1/3. (middle) f = 1. (right) f = 3.)

If a static camera observes a static scene while the focal length $f$ changes over time, a motion field is produced despite the lack of physical motion. The temporal derivative of the perspective projection equation yields:

$$\begin{aligned}
\begin{bmatrix}
\dot{x} \\
\dot{y}
\end{bmatrix}
= 
\frac{\dot{f}}{f}
\begin{bmatrix}
x \\
y
\end{bmatrix}
\end{aligned}$$

This results in a scaling (zoom and crop) of the image. Because the motion field does not depend on the 3D scene structure $Z$, varying the focal length from a single viewpoint does not provide depth information. This demonstrates that a 2D motion field can exist even in the absence of scene or camera motion.

The **cross-attention** layer (`cross-attn`) addresses the question of how to insert image tokens into a text model. The answer is to let the text tokens attend to the image tokens. The image tokens emit their value vectors, which are mixed with the text token value vectors according to the attention weights. The equations for a cross-attention layer are:
$$\begin{aligned}
    \mathbf{Q}_{t} = \mathbf{T}_{t}\mathbf{W}_{t,q}^\mathsf{T}\\
    \mathbf{K}_{\ell} = \mathbf{T}_{\ell}\mathbf{W}_{\ell,k}^\mathsf{T}\\
    \mathbf{V}_{\ell} = \mathbf{T}_{\ell}\mathbf{W}_{\ell,v}^\mathsf{T}\\
    \mathbf{A}_{\texttt{cross}} = \texttt{softmax}(\frac{\mathbf{Q}_{t}\mathbf{K}_{\ell}^\mathsf{T}}{\sqrt{m}})\\
    \mathbf{T}_{\texttt{out}, \texttt{cross}} = \mathbf{A}_{\texttt{cross}}\mathbf{V}_{\ell}
\end{aligned}$$
where variables subscripted with $t$ are associated with text tokens and variables subscripted with $\ell$ are associated with image tokens. The output can be combined with self-attention via weighted summation:
$$\begin{aligned}
    \mathbf{T} = w_{\texttt{cross}}\mathbf{T}_{\texttt{out}, \texttt{cross}}  + w_{\texttt{self}}\mathbf{T}_{\texttt{out}, \texttt{self}}
\end{aligned}$$
where $\mathbf{T}_{\texttt{out}, \texttt{self}}$ is regular self-attention on the input text token $\mathbf{T}_t$.

### Adapting the Output

MiDaS returns disparity values up to an unknown constant ($d_0$), which must be transformed into world coordinates ($X,Y,Z$) for each pixel. To translate the returned disparity $d[n,m]$ into depth values $z[n,m]$, we invert the disparity after adding the constant:

$$\begin{aligned}z [n,m ] = \frac{1}{ d [n,m ] + d_0}
\end{aligned}$$

Assuming a pinhole camera and using the depth $z[n,m]$ and the camera matrix $\mathbf{K}$, we obtain the 3D coordinates in the camera reference frame as:

$$\begin{aligned}
\begin{bmatrix}
X \\
Y \\ 
Z 
\end{bmatrix}
= z[n,m ] \times \mathbf{K}^{-1} \times
\begin{bmatrix}
n \\
m \\ 
1 
\end{bmatrix}
\end{aligned}$$

The camera matrix $\mathbf{K}$ accounts for focal length, image size, camera center, and rotation: $\mathbf{K} = \mathbf{M} \mathbf{R}$. The intrinsic parameters $\mathbf{M}$ are defined by:

$$\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{M}
= 
\begin{bmatrix}
a & 0 & n_0\\
0 & a & m_0 \\ 
0 & 0 & 1 
\end{bmatrix}.
\end{aligned}$$

[FIGURE:2c6cb957a032955d]

The extrinsic parameters $\mathbf{R}$ model the 3D rotation, primarily around the $X$-axis:

$$\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{R}
= 
\begin{bmatrix}
1 & 0 & 0\\
0 & \cos(\theta) & -\sin(\theta) \\ 
0 & \sin(\theta) & \cos(\theta) 
\end{bmatrix}.
\end{aligned}$$

To apply this, we use heuristic settings: $d_0 = 0$, $n_0 = N/2$, $m_0 = M/2$, and $a = N$ (where $N$ is image width).

